\section{Conclusion}

\vspace{1cm}

The visit to ISKCON Temple was not just a field activity, but a meaningful experience that helped us connect Universal Human Values with real-life practice. Being in a spiritual environment allowed us to observe how discipline, compassion, and responsibility are not just theoretical concepts but values that can be lived every day. The organized functioning of the temple, along with the calm and devoted behavior of the people, created an atmosphere of harmony and positivity.

\vspace{1cm}

One of the most important realizations from this visit was the concept of selfless action, which is strongly emphasized in the Bhagavad Gita. The teaching “You have a right to perform your duties, but not to the fruits of your actions” (Chapter 2, Verse 47) deeply connects with the way temple volunteers serve without expecting anything in return. This helped us understand that true service comes from dedication and not from the desire for rewards.

\vspace{1cm}

Another key learning was about maintaining inner balance and calmness in all situations. The teaching of Lord Krishna that one should remain steady in success and failure (Chapter 2, Verse 48) was clearly reflected during the crowded festive situation we experienced. Despite delays and challenges, maintaining patience and composure allowed us to complete our tasks effectively.

\vspace{1cm}

The visit also helped us understand the importance of duty and righteousness. The idea that one should perform their responsibilities sincerely, as highlighted in Chapter 3 of the Bhagavad Gita, was reflected in both our team and the temple management. Each individual performed their role with dedication, contributing to the smooth functioning of the entire system.

\vspace{1cm}

In conclusion, this experience strengthened our understanding of Universal Human Values by connecting them with spirituality and real-life situations. It taught us that values like compassion, responsibility, cooperation, patience, and respect are essential for leading a balanced and meaningful life. Inspired by the teachings of Lord Krishna, we aim to apply these values in our daily lives and become more responsible and aware individuals.
